00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:04.288
We wanted to make sure that
we could work with things

00:08:04.288 --> 00:08:08.050
like Spanner, if we needed
transactional activity,

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:10.330
and Pub/Sub for that messaging.

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:12.040
But if you notice
the internal systems,

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.990
as well, so whatever
we built had

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:16.960
to work with both
of these paradigms.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:22.990
Also, on your left-hand side--
forgetting my left and right--

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:25.250
we have the user
side of the house.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:29.500
So this is converting what
a user says into actions.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.180
We use Dialogflow for that.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.880
We used Firebase to actually
get the information that's

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:36.730
been retrieved to the user.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:40.432
If you notice, we've had this--
we built this SubHub service.

00:08:40.432 --> 00:08:41.890
Now, what that hub
does is actually

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:45.190
coordinate and interact all of
these communication channels.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:48.310
The reason we did that is
because we wanted easy drop in

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:50.330
and out of other components.

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.640
So for example,
we used Firebase,

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.070
but internally,
within Citi, they

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.210
have other systems that
can update clients.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.180
So the system needed
to be able to deal

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:02.980
with two types of things
very easily within any part

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:04.624
of this component architecture.

00:09:04.624 --> 00:09:06.290
And actually, looking
towards the future

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:11.207
was other things that we'd
like to add to the platform.

00:09:11.207 --> 00:09:12.040
JAMES TROMANS: Sure.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:12.539
Yeah.

00:09:12.539 --> 00:09:15.930
So when we think
about what Dialogflow

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:17.890
is going to be doing
for us, Dialogflow

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:21.340
serves the sit between
the inquiries from a user,

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.170
and then what you
actually want to serve

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.370
is a fulfillment
request to the user.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:27.370
And so I'm interested
in saying, OK, we

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:28.870
have all of this
proprietary data.

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:31.203
But we also have a lot of
proprietary algorithms, right?

00:09:31.203 --> 00:09:31.870
We're a bank.

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:34.510
And so if I have a user
that's interested in asking

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:37.160
quite a challenging question
that's going to take a Quant

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.150
library that we've built
over 15 years to answer,

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:42.250
that's a very much
custom-crafted modeled

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:44.710
approach, which we have on
premise working already.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.300
So we need to integrate the two.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:47.740
But of course, as
we move forward,

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:48.850
and as we're
already doing today,

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:51.100
we're interested in using
more modern machine learning

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.200
algorithms from open
source libraries,

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.100
whether that's actually Boost
or Scikit-learn or TensorFlow.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.716
Whatever we're going to service
the request from the client

00:09:59.716 --> 00:10:01.090
with, whatever
the inference tool

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:03.357
that we're going
to use to do that,

00:10:03.357 --> 00:10:04.690
it needs to work there, as well.

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:06.730
So you've got
completely custom stuff

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:10.610
on premise, a middle ground with
reusing open source technology.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.169
And then, obviously, we'd be
silly not to think forward

00:10:13.169 --> 00:10:15.460
to the future, where we might
be using some of Google's

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:18.520
hosted model solutions, so some
of the existing off-the-shelf

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:21.690
managed solutions from a machine
learning perspective, as well.

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:22.490
REZA ROKNI: OK.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.730
So obviously, there's
a lot of technology

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.160
that we used in building
out this application--

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:30.414
Containers, BigQuery,
Spanner, Firebase.

