One is BigQuery, as one
of our data sources.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.750
And the other one is
obviously Dialogflow itself.

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:48.979
So just a little
bit about BigQuery,

00:10:48.979 --> 00:10:50.270
if you're not familiar already.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:54.100
It's Google's petabyte-scale
data warehouse in the cloud.

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:56.620
Like any data warehouse,
it is ANSI SQL.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:00.040
It has the ability to
build dashboards or run

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:01.630
ad hoc queries.

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:03.850
It also has a
streaming API, which

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.280
means you can actually
upsert streams of information

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:10.180
into BigQuery, and when you make
a call, it's up-to-date data.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.400
And that will come
in useful in a moment

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:14.560
when we show some of the demos.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.237
The other part of it is
fully managed and serverless.

00:11:17.237 --> 00:11:19.570
In other words, the bank
doesn't need to set up anything

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:21.130
to make use of it.

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:24.100
So one component, and part of
the architecture for BigQuery,

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:26.290
which was particularly
helpful here,

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:29.765
is this idea of separation
of storage from processing.

00:11:29.765 --> 00:11:31.890
Now, what that means,
there's two benefits for you.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:35.290
So one, in terms of processing,
if you're not running a query,

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:38.110
then you're not paying
for any machines

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:41.800
to be ready for
accepting that query.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.641
But the other benefit is
around sharing of data.

00:11:44.641 --> 00:11:46.390
So if you think of a
traditional database,

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:48.490
you have the database
software that you install.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:51.310
You attached SAN, NAS
disk, et cetera, to that.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:52.720
Then you load data into it.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:54.862
Now to share data, you
either have to give access

00:11:54.862 --> 00:11:56.570
to the database-- and
I used to be a DBA,

00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:58.930
and that's not something
I'd give very easily--

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:01.360
or you need to build
an application layer,

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.750
or you need to do the FTP
dance, which is never pretty.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:07.150
However, because
of this separation,

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:10.030
what you can have is
data stored in a project,

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:13.030
as long as the correct security
and ACLs have been done,

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:14.880
can be shared with
other projects.

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:16.900
So within an internal,
very large organization,

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:19.900
like Citi, if they're
uploading data, they can--

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:21.930
again, with the right
security and ACL levels--

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.710
share data amongst themselves.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:28.030
But also, it allows you to blend
in data from external sources

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:28.960
very easily.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:30.980
And a very good example
of this is the work

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:32.650
that we did with
Thomson Reuters,

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:34.330
with quite a few banks.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:36.910
So obviously, Thomson
Reuters have a huge data set

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:39.490
that is available through
their web service APIs.

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:41.185
But for POCs, they
also worked with us

00:12:41.185 --> 00:12:44.770
with providing that data
onto BigQuery for the banks

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:46.030
to consume.

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:49.250
And that gives us access to 20
years of historical tick data.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.320
They also do near real time.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:56.590
So within seconds, they can
do inserts into our database.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.140
And again, the benefit
here is that, once you've

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:01.390
gone through the right
security and the access levels,

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:02.764
there's nothing
else for the bank

00:13:02.764 --> 00:13:05.520
to do other than run queries.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:08.170
What I'm going to do is
just do a quick demo.

00:13:08.170 --> 00:13:11.350
We can go over to
the laptop, please.

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:13.150
Thank you.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:15.249
That is a giant screen.

00:13:15.249 --> 00:13:16.540
I better not make any mistakes.

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:17.039
OK.

00:13:17.039 --> 00:13:21.130
So this is the new
BigQuery user interface.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:23.890
I'm not going to run through
all of the capabilities here.

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:26.260
I just wanted to highlight
one particular thing,

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:28.460
and that is that we are
currently in this project--

00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:31.795
FX project-- which is the one
I've used to do my develop

00:13:31.795 --> 00:13:34.690
and testing with for James.

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:37.420
And within this project,
on the left-hand side,

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:41.020
we can see other projects that
are available because I've

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:42.030
got access to them.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:43.450
I've been authorized
against them.

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:46.040
So obviously, there's
the project that I'm in.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:49.420
But further down, we can
see this TR-EMS project.

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:53.170
Now this is a project
that Thomson Reuters own,

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:56.866
and they have given me access to
data sets within that project.

00:13:56.866 --> 00:13:58.240
I'm going to just
quickly go down

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:01.090
to one data set, which is
New York Stock Exchange

00:14:01.090 --> 00:14:02.890
information.

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:05.180
Here, we have a table.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.330
I'm just going to
select Details.

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:11.200
So this table is five
terabytes and 40 billion rows

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:12.350
of information.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:14.440
Now, for me to run
a query, I'm going

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:19.820
to run this query on the
top here against that data.

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:21.700
So I'm running this
query in my project.

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:23.176
I haven't moved any data around.

00:14:23.176 --> 00:14:24.550
And it's working
against the data

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:26.716
that I've been authorized
against in Thomson Reuters

00:14:26.716 --> 00:14:27.550
project.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:30.010
So that will take
about 25 to 30 seconds.

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:32.767
That particular query,
volume-weighted average price,

00:14:32.767 --> 00:14:34.850
you wouldn't normally run
it against all the data.

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.850
I'm just doing that to
show that we can do it

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:38.380
against 30 billion rows.

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:42.380
Takes about 25 to 27
seconds to complete.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:45.280
So there was no need for me to
do my tap dance routine, which

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.110
is very good for you.

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:51.022
Here, we see 63
was the result. I'm

00:14:51.022 --> 00:14:52.480
going to just show
one other table.

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:54.520
I won't run a query against it.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:56.640
And that is in
this data set 213,

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:00.880
which has FX currency data.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:03.970
And just the thing to highlight
from the details, if I

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:05.980
can get my--

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:07.300
move this properly.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:09.220
This particular table
has a streaming buffer.

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:11.770
What that means is that
Thomson Reuters is currently

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:14.710
pushing data into
this table, and it

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:17.550
is being updated in near
real time, within seconds.

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.300
So when a query is run
against that table,

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:21.200
we get live update information.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:21.700
OK.

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:25.240
So with that, if we could go
back to the slides, please.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:28.440
So that's one of the data sets
that we're going to be using.

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:30.950
Thank you.

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:33.339
So now, towards Dialogflow.

00:15:33.339 --> 00:15:34.880
As I said, I've been
here five years,

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:39.800
and it's really great to see all
the icons popping in, and how

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:41.690
full these charts are becoming.

00:15:41.690 --> 00:15:45.890
So out of our Cloud
AI solution set here,

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:48.560
we are going to be
talking about Dialogflow,

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:50.990
which allows us to build
a conversational interface

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:53.960
against--

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:56.450
when users ask for
information, we

00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:59.849
can extract information from
the intents that they have.

00:15:59.849 --> 00:16:01.640
And one of the nice
things about Dialogflow

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:05.390
is that it is making more
and more use of all the ML

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:08.854
capabilities that we have
built into the product itself.

00:16:08.854 --> 00:16:10.520
So natural language
understanding, so we

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:12.860
have sentiment
analysis now within it.

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:14.180
We have speech-to-text.

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:16.250
All of these components
are just coming out

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:19.250
of the box with Dialogflow.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:22.777
In terms of the use cases that
we are going to talk about,

00:16:22.777 --> 00:16:24.860
the one that we predominately
worked with James on

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:27.180
was around the
business to employees.

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:31.460
So this is helping the
traders make decisions.

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:34.460
And we're looking in
the future, potentially,

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:39.604
towards doing stuff
around their customers.

00:16:39.604 --> 00:16:41.270
In terms of Dialogflow
itself, and we'll

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:43.370
go through this in a
little bit more detail

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:45.140
when we do the demo,
there is an agent.

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:49.160
You can try and think of
an agent as a NLU module.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:53.240
It's an encapsulation
of entities, intents,

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:54.470
and contexts.

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:57.950
What an intent is is when a
user provides some information,

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:01.100
like says a sentence,
the intent allows

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:02.780
us to map that
sentence to actions

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:04.530
that we're going to be taking.

00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:06.260
What entities do--
so often, when

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:08.390
we want to take an action
against a sentence,

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:11.210
we need to extract information,
and entities allows

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:13.160
us to extract those parameters.

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:17.480
A context gives us where we are
in the conversational space.

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:20.390
So if a user, for
example, has currently

00:17:20.390 --> 00:17:23.420
asked for information about
eurodollar currencies,

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:25.670
and then the next
question is give me news,

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.069
then we know, because they
were talking about eurodollar,

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:31.860
that that's what they
intend us to go and fetch.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:33.710
And then we have the
fulfillment service,

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:36.410
which allows us to make calls
to all these other back end

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:37.590
services.

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:39.950
And with that, we're
going to go now

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:45.949
to a demo of the client working
within the Citi environment.

00:17:45.949 --> 00:17:46.990
JAMES TROMANS: All right.

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:56.250
So just before I let it run,
I'm going to just orientate

00:17:56.250 --> 00:17:57.860
to what you're seeing here.

00:17:57.860 --> 00:17:59.510
Hopefully, you can read that.

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:02.067
So on the right-hand
side of the screen,

00:18:02.067 --> 00:18:03.900
or I should say the
right 2/3 of the screen,

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.930
is an existing internal
application within Citi.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:08.240
We call it the trader toolkit.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:10.680
And for the purposes
of this demo,

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:14.230
I produced a simple dashboard,
which comprises two widgets.

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:16.230
One is a chart widget,
which is in the top half,

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:17.800
and the other is a table.

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:20.640
And that table
contains interesting

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.560
economic, macroeconomic
events that

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:25.762
are relevant for the particular
asset that's being plotted.

00:18:25.762 --> 00:18:27.720
On the left-hand side,
you'll see that we have,

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:30.300
if you're familiar with
Dialogflow already,

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.610
an out-of-the-box chat UI.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:36.700
Now, obviously, you'd integrate
this with your own chat system,

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:39.420
if you were going to embed
this into your product.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:41.700
So Citi has some of
its own technology,

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:42.879
which this would embed to.

00:18:42.879 --> 00:18:44.670
And of course, anything
that offers an API,

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:45.789
this could work with.

00:18:45.789 --> 00:18:47.330
So I'm going to set
the demo to play.

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:53.060
So we recorded this
approximately a week

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:54.840
and a half ago, I think.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:55.600
When did we do it?

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:57.580
The 16th or the 17th.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:18:59.080
And so the user on
the left is going

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.560
to ask to draw a
chart of euro dollar.

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:05.822
So once they did that,
immediately, in real time--

00:19:05.822 --> 00:19:07.030
we haven't sped anything up--

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:08.991
the client application
on the right-hand side

00:19:08.991 --> 00:19:11.240
took that information and
was able to change the chart

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:12.670
in the top half of the screen.

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:16.522
And the second, bottom half of
the screen updated, as well.

00:19:16.522 --> 00:19:17.980
And so in order
for that to happen,

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:19.900
two things needed
to take place--

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:22.992
the idea of drawing a chart,
this intent, and the entities

00:19:22.992 --> 00:19:23.950
needed to be extracted.

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:26.920
In this case, eurodollar, or
the euro dollar exchange rate.

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:28.840
You'll see these circles
on the chart, which

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:30.640
show the relevant
economic events,

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.640
and the colors of the circles
depict how important they are,

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:35.140
and the number is
kind of the weight

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:36.970
of how important that event is.

00:19:36.970 --> 00:19:40.160
And so the user can now,
interactively, say, OK.

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:41.144
How about dollar yen?

00:19:41.144 --> 00:19:43.060
Different currency pair,
the Japanese currency

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:44.130
against the dollar.

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:45.671
And so you'll notice
that they didn't

00:19:45.671 --> 00:19:47.620
have to say chart,
or show me something.

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:49.360
It's just very conversational.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:51.250
It's carried forward
the context of the fact

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.100
that the user's
interested in charting.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:54.280
And it's obviously
understood that it's

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:55.690
extracting a different entity--

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:57.140
in this case, dollar yen.

00:19:57.140 --> 00:19:59.260
So the user can now interact
with the application.

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:00.218
They can fiddle around.

00:20:00.218 --> 00:20:03.260
They can explore and
do some analysis.

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:06.172
So it's getting slightly
more complex now.

00:20:06.172 --> 00:20:07.130
We can do other things.

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:08.840
So we can have other
types of intents.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:11.590
So the user is now saying,
OK, please scatterplot euro

00:20:11.590 --> 00:20:12.442
against yen.

00:20:12.442 --> 00:20:14.650
And because the application,
again, is context aware,

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:16.441
it knows that the user
is intending to plot

00:20:16.441 --> 00:20:18.320
euro dollar against dollar yen.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.020
So here is an
interactive scatterplot,

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:23.586
the x-axis being euro dollar
and the y-axis being dollar yen.

00:20:23.586 --> 00:20:25.210
And some of this
existing functionality

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:26.710
that we have within the
application, the user

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:29.380
can play around and, instead of
plotting just the raw values,

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:31.130
they can plot things
like percentage rank,

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:33.255
and they can change the
lookback windows over which

00:20:33.255 --> 00:20:35.000
that's calculated, and so on.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.700
So we've got simple
charting intents.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:39.270
We've got slightly
more plotting intents

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:40.824
embedded into this application.

00:20:40.824 --> 00:20:41.740
But we can go further.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:43.190
We can do more.

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:44.860
So the next one I
want to show you

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:47.750
is this concept of a
backtesting intent.

00:20:47.750 --> 00:20:51.952
So the user is now
going to say, backtest--

00:20:51.952 --> 00:20:52.660
what did they do?

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:57.370
Euro dollar after dollar
yen is greater than 110.

00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:59.129
So currency rates
obviously have values,

00:20:59.129 --> 00:21:00.670
and the user is
interested in knowing

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:03.128
what happens to one currency
when a different currency goes

00:21:03.128 --> 00:21:04.432
above a certain value.

00:21:04.432 --> 00:21:05.890
And so in order
for this to happen,

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:09.770
we didn't just have to get
the intent and the entities.

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:11.350
We also had to have
this condition.

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:13.780
And so it's brought up
this study of what happens.

00:21:13.780 --> 00:21:17.110
And you'll see at the top,
we have this time series,

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.370
and the blue dots represent
every time that event occurred.

00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:23.020
And in the middle chart, we
have the average price path

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:25.450
up to 24 hours
later, showing you

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:27.452
what's happened to the
euro dollar exchange rate

00:21:27.452 --> 00:21:29.410
after the dollar yen has
gone greater than 110.

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:32.420
As you click in, you get the
distribution of returns below.

00:21:32.420 --> 00:21:34.630
This is a nice
study for the user

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:36.430
to be able to interact with.

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:38.504
So shifting gears
slightly, moving away

00:21:38.504 --> 00:21:40.420
from just controlling
the user interface, what

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:42.370
happens if we actually wanted
to engage with our customers

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:44.162
so that they could
buy something from us?

00:21:44.162 --> 00:21:46.120
We have a lot of custom
lingo in finance, which

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:47.203
you'll see some of it now.

00:21:47.203 --> 00:21:50.930
So the user is asking to chart
euro, euro against the dollar.

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:52.610
That's the standard
when they say that.

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:54.050
And then they're going
to ask for a price.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:56.170
So the way in which a
customer or a sales person

00:21:56.170 --> 00:21:58.150
may choose to do that
is quite specific.

00:21:58.150 --> 00:21:59.551
They say, how wide in 9 million.

00:21:59.551 --> 00:22:01.300
So they're interested
in a price that we'd

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:03.240
be willing, as a
bank, to buy to them--

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:05.220
sorry, sell to them,
and buy back from them

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:06.400
9 million eurodollar.

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:08.920
And we only quote the last two
digits of the exchange rate.

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:10.180
We have these conventions.

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:13.150
So we're showing 49 on one
side and 51 on the other.

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:14.410
And the user's saying, mine.

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:15.592
That's lingo for buy.

00:22:15.592 --> 00:22:16.300
They want to buy.

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:18.679
So they're interested in
picking one of those prices.

00:22:18.679 --> 00:22:20.470
They didn't tell us,
in advance, which one.

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:22.060
They want to buy it, and
so they've said mine,

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:24.180
and we've returned with
transaction complete.

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:25.554
And here's an
example transaction

00:22:25.554 --> 00:22:28.390
ID, explaining to them
what they've done.

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:30.340
So following on with
this kind of idea

00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:32.800
of having to train up
on different lingo,

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:34.050
we have this concept of cable.

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:35.380
So if you're not
from finance, cable

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:37.296
is the great British
pound against the dollar.

00:22:37.296 --> 00:22:40.040
It's a specific terminology
just for that pair.

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:41.460
And so they've
asked to plot that.

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:43.210
And then they're going
to, instead of buy,

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:46.570
this time the user's going to
say, OK, make me 3 million,

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:48.630
a different way of
asking for the price.

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:50.845
Instead of how wide, they've
said make me 3 million.

00:22:50.845 --> 00:22:52.720
So we've given back them
a quote specifically

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:53.982
for 3 million cable.

00:22:53.982 --> 00:22:56.190
And it's the same price
because we haven't integrated

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:59.239
this with our pricing API, but
that would be the next step.

00:22:59.239 --> 00:23:01.030
And then they've said,
done left-hand side,

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:02.800
and that means that they're
interested in selling,

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:03.790
and they've done so.

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:06.260
So the transaction
has completed.

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:09.340
So just to kind of summarize
what's taken place here,

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:11.090
we've controlled the
UI to enable the user

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:13.810
to do some exploratory
data analysis,

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:16.510
look around at charts, look at
the relevant economic events,

00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:17.621
do some backtesting.

00:23:17.621 --> 00:23:19.120
And then, having
done that analysis,

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:20.786
they've decided that
they wanted to move

00:23:20.786 --> 00:23:24.070
into a transactional
business engagement with us.

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:26.710
And we've trained Dialogflow
on very customized,

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:29.530
specific terminology, and we'll
talk a little bit about how

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:30.660
you do that in a minute.

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.230
Over to Rez for another demo.

00:23:32.230 --> 00:23:32.510
REZA ROKNI: Thank you.

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:34.051
Can you switch to
the laptop, please?

00:23:34.051 --> 00:23:35.080
Thank you.

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:36.520
So obviously, I
don't have access

00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:37.910
to their internal systems.

00:23:37.910 --> 00:23:40.960
So when I'm playing and trying
to explore stuff for James,

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:44.325
we built a sort
of parallel agent.

00:23:44.325 --> 00:23:46.450
And I have no talent in
UI, so one of my colleagues

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:48.190
in Singapore built this for us.

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:50.660
In principle, it's the
same kind of thing.

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:53.360
It has a graph of information.

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:55.120
We also have some
news information

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:58.910
underneath that we populate
from Thomson Reuters again.

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:00.760
The data that we've
provided for is--

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:04.540
this is 2015 that we have
access to from Thomson Reuters.

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:06.250
So what I'm going
to do is just show

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:10.960
that this is fairly similar to
the application we had before.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:13.040
Here, we have
sterling being shown.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:22.110
So I'm going to do, show me
a graph of a euro dollar.

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:25.210
And when I go back, that
should have changed.

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:28.610
And let's see how
dry my mouth is.

00:24:28.610 --> 00:24:31.170
We shall soon find out.

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:32.020
[PHONE DINGS]

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:35.246
Show me a graph
of pound sterling.

00:24:35.246 --> 00:24:36.162
[PHONE DINGS]

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:39.250
There you go.

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:45.930
And [PHONE DINGS] show me
news on pound sterling.

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:46.910
[PHONE DINGS]

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:48.370
OK.

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:49.676
So my mouth isn't that dry.

00:24:49.676 --> 00:24:51.550
So that actually changed
underneath, as well.

00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:53.620
Sorry I didn't have
that highlighted.

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:55.960
That was just Dialogflow.

00:24:55.960 --> 00:24:57.870
The same intents
were being used.

00:24:57.870 --> 00:24:59.440
We're now using
the speech-to-text

00:24:59.440 --> 00:25:01.930
to actually convert what
I was saying into text.

00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:05.860
That was being
represented on the graph.

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:07.570
So in terms of
Dialogflow itself--

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:09.910
just to go through a
few pieces before we

00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:13.210
do another small demo
around some new features

00:25:13.210 --> 00:25:16.840
that have been
added to Dialogflow.

00:25:16.840 --> 00:25:19.330
This is the Dialogflow
user interface.

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:21.460
I'm currently in my
FX tester environment,

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:25.150
which is just a play environment
to work around with intents.

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:28.030
For those of you not
familiar with Dialogflow,

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:30.730
we have the idea of intents
to actually convert text

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:32.380
into actions and entities.

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:35.050
What I usually start
with is entities.

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:38.560
In this case, the entities
that we have available

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:41.800
are things that are built
in, including currency.

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:45.280
Now, the currency
entity, Dialogflow

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:47.590
does have a system-built
entity called

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:50.320
Currency that would normally
suffice for any currency

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:50.900
activity.

00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:53.050
However, as James
demonstrated, there's

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:56.870
a lot of domain-specific
language that we use here.

00:25:56.870 --> 00:25:58.870
And therefore, I built
my own currency entity

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:01.360
so I could overload it with
the domain-specific language

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:02.630
that we need.

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:08.726
For example, if we go
to Australian dollar,

00:26:08.726 --> 00:26:10.100
so by doing that,
I've brought up

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:11.190
the entity Australian dollar.

00:26:11.190 --> 00:26:13.150
This is the symbol and
these are the synonyms

00:26:13.150 --> 00:26:14.440
that are associated with it.

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:17.890
At the end, we
have Aussie as one

00:26:17.890 --> 00:26:21.000
of the domain-specific
languages.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.750
GBP-- so I'm going
to add one to this.

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:27.047
So this is Great British pounds.

00:26:27.047 --> 00:26:29.130
And where I grew up in
Northolt, we don't actually

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:30.660
refer to pounds as pounds.

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:33.330
Down where I work,
it's quids, mate.

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:36.319
Like two quid for a
pint, showing my age.

00:26:36.319 --> 00:26:38.610
It's been a long time since
it was two quid for a pint.

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:40.680
So for that, I'm
going to put quid.

00:26:43.144 --> 00:26:44.560
And essentially,
that's all I have

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:49.780
to do to actually teach it that
quid equates to pound sterling.

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:51.910
Now, when I go to do an intent--

00:26:51.910 --> 00:26:53.380
so we have various intents.

00:26:53.380 --> 00:26:54.880
I'm just going to show
one of the intents, which

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:56.713
is the graph intent,
which you've seen quite

00:26:56.713 --> 00:26:58.510
a few times in the demo now.

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:02.050
This graph intent takes
a series of phrases.

00:27:02.050 --> 00:27:04.030
I'm going to put one in now.

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:11.520
Show me a graph of
quid versus dollar.

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:17.960
So I just added quid as a
synonym for that entity.

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:20.390
What Dialogflow does
very nicely for me

00:27:20.390 --> 00:27:21.770
is, when I type
that sentence in,

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:24.200
it's already figured out that
some of the values that I've

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:25.720
put in are currencies.

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:27.980
It's already highlighted
those for me.

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:31.610
And this is step one of
extracting the parameters

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:34.200
that we need from this intent.

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:36.680
So if I scroll down,
we can actually

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.450
see the actions and parameters.

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:40.790
Here, I've defined the
input graph action.

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:44.630
This is call to my code at the
back end to actually invoke

00:27:44.630 --> 00:27:46.310
that handler.

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:49.400
We also have some parameters,
so for example, the currency

00:27:49.400 --> 00:27:50.700
parameter.

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:52.280
If you notice on
this side, we've

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:54.140
ticked this box under Required.

00:27:54.140 --> 00:27:56.210
So if a user says
show me a graph,

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.230
and they don't provide
a currency to me,

00:27:58.230 --> 00:27:59.390
I don't know what to draw.

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:02.440
So we're going to prompt
them to give us the currency.

00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:05.000
And that's all inbuilt
into Dialogflow.

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:07.340
If I click on the prompts,
these are a couple

00:28:07.340 --> 00:28:09.570
of the prompts that will
be provided to the user,

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:11.695
as we get them to give us
the currencies that we're

00:28:11.695 --> 00:28:12.631
interested in.

00:28:12.631 --> 00:28:14.630
If you notice, we've added
more than one prompt.

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:17.327
This is good UI design
for conversations

00:28:17.327 --> 00:28:19.910
to make the user feel a little
bit more comfortable with using

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:22.250
the chatbot, if you
give different sentences

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:25.340
for the same questions.

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:28.160
Outside of this piece,
I'm going to just show

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:30.080
a new piece of
functionality that

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.070
has been added to Dialogflow.

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:36.720
So I'm going to go to one of
my user environments, so FX.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:39.690
This is where we've actually
got things wired up.

00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:42.560
And one of the things I find
particularly useful when

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:45.410
working and developing
on here is the test

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:47.970
UI that you can use on
the right-hand side.

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:53.850
So here, if I do show me a
graph of yen versus dollar,

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:57.570
what this is doing is we can
see the response that was given.

00:28:57.570 --> 00:28:59.760
But much more
interestingly, it gives us

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:01.500
information about
what Dialogflow

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:03.120
extracted from that sentence.

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:06.610
So I can see the action
is correct-- input.graph.

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:08.430
I can see that the
parameter is currency,

00:29:08.430 --> 00:29:10.950
and it's picked
out yen US dollar.

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:14.540
We have some extra information,
which is not part of the demo

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:15.690
today.

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:18.120
Also particularly useful
is diagnostic info.

00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:22.230
So here, we actually see
all the raw JSON responses

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:24.810
that it is sending to
the back end services.

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:27.660
And this is super useful when
you're doing debug and testing.

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:30.420
Just take the JSON from
there, put it into a message,

00:29:30.420 --> 00:29:32.317
and then call your
back end web service.

00:29:32.317 --> 00:29:33.900
So the thing I'm
going to show you now

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:35.970
is a little bit of a
different flow from the one

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:37.380
that we saw with James.

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:38.490
So I've got a currency.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:43.090
Now I'm going to say,
quote me 2 million.

00:29:43.090 --> 00:29:46.120
My one is not as
integrated as James's.

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:50.650
So I just say, here's a
quote for 2 million xx yy.

00:29:50.650 --> 00:29:53.025
If you notice, however,
there is a sentiment score

00:29:53.025 --> 00:29:53.650
attached to it.

00:29:53.650 --> 00:29:55.220
This has been built
into Dialogflow.

00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:56.844
I think it was just
recently announced.

00:29:56.844 --> 00:30:00.190
And this provides a negative
or positive sentiment

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:01.480
to the statement given.

00:30:01.480 --> 00:30:03.670
Now, that statement that
I asked, give me a quote,

00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:05.110
didn't have any sentiment.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:08.290
Let's say I was not
happy with that price.

00:30:08.290 --> 00:30:10.880
And I'm British, so I'm not
going to swear at the system.

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:17.170
So we're going to say, that's
a terrible, awful price,

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:20.350
which is as strong as I
like to get, being English.

00:30:20.350 --> 00:30:24.490
Here, we can actually see
that the response was, oh,

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:25.990
please don't get mad.

00:30:25.990 --> 00:30:30.700
Now, obviously, Citi would put
a far more professional response

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:31.510
to that thing.

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:34.160
But the reason it said
that is, if I scroll down,

00:30:34.160 --> 00:30:37.760
we have a sentiment
score of 0.9 negative.

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:40.750
Now, if I go to some
of my code to show

00:30:40.750 --> 00:30:45.910
how we implemented this,
what we do is that intent--

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:47.860
the handler for that intent--

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:50.410
looks at the sentiment
score for that sentence.

00:30:50.410 --> 00:30:52.535
If it goes over a
threshold-- and you'll

00:30:52.535 --> 00:30:54.160
notice that the
threshold is quite low.

00:30:54.160 --> 00:30:56.680
I've deliberately set that--

00:30:56.680 --> 00:30:59.691
it's going to fire a different
response to the user, instead

00:30:59.691 --> 00:31:00.940
of sorry we couldn't help you.

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:02.620
Oh, please don't get mad.

00:31:02.620 --> 00:31:05.080
Now, the other
thing that it does

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:09.700
is it logs to Pub/Sub,
which is our global publish

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:13.870
and subscribe API, the message
with the negative sentiment.

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:16.990
And what this allows us
to do is to have systems

00:31:16.990 --> 00:31:20.200
that can react to if a
customer, for example,

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:21.730
is getting angry at us.

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:23.310
Now, the sentiment
score I showed

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:24.890
you was part of that sentence.

00:31:24.890 --> 00:31:26.890
However, you can have an
overall sentiment score

00:31:26.890 --> 00:31:28.420
for the whole conversation.

00:31:28.420 --> 00:31:30.790
And this starts getting
very, very interesting,

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:34.270
in the kind of things that we
can now do with the chatbot.

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:36.430
The other thing of
integration that we built

00:31:36.430 --> 00:31:40.450
was all of the intents
I log to BigQuery.

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:46.900
Now, the results, I'm
just dumping as raw JSON.

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:49.880
BigQuery can extract
JSON information.

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:52.804
So every intent, everything
that's being done,

00:31:52.804 --> 00:31:53.470
is being logged.

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:55.840
If I run the query, it
actually will show just

00:31:55.840 --> 00:31:58.120
what's at the bottom anyway.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:00.880
But here, we have the
different intents,

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:03.400
a confidence score
in how confident

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:05.500
we were that that was
the intent the user had,

00:32:05.500 --> 00:32:09.200
and the sentiment score
that was associated with it.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:11.050
Now, the reason
this is interesting

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:14.800
is if we have a mechanism
for Pub/Sub to notify us

00:32:14.800 --> 00:32:17.170
something's going wrong,
and we have the history

00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:18.400
of the conversation, we can--

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:19.180
JAMES TROMANS: Right, yeah.

00:32:19.180 --> 00:32:20.763
So you can imagine,
if you're building

00:32:20.763 --> 00:32:24.760
this for your own teams, as I
was thinking about it at Citi.

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:26.710
If you're automatically
serving requests

00:32:26.710 --> 00:32:28.810
from customers--
in our case, it's

00:32:28.810 --> 00:32:31.180
both internal
users and external.

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:33.370
Especially in the case
of external customers,

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:34.630
you want to be thinking
about, OK, we're

00:32:34.630 --> 00:32:35.780
automatically serving them.

00:32:35.780 --> 00:32:38.140
But what happens if
something goes wrong?

00:32:38.140 --> 00:32:39.760
And it might not
even have gone wrong.

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:43.150
Perhaps just the customer's
dissatisfied with something

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:45.560
when they came to you, even
before the conversation.

00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:47.890
And so you may choose to
actually have something

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:49.490
published onto Pub/Sub.

00:32:49.490 --> 00:32:50.770
Obviously, that's real time.

00:32:50.770 --> 00:32:53.110
And you can surface
an alert to a user--

00:32:53.110 --> 00:32:55.840
sorry, a Citi
employee, in my case.

00:32:55.840 --> 00:32:58.840
You can actually then field that
request manually and slot back

00:32:58.840 --> 00:33:00.500
into the chat and take control.

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:03.490
And by publishing all
of the previous requests

00:33:03.490 --> 00:33:05.020
through the
Dialogflow interface,

00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:08.169
whether that's your own or
something else, into BigQuery,

00:33:08.169 --> 00:33:09.460
you obviously have the history.

00:33:09.460 --> 00:33:11.324
So although we didn't
build it for the demo,

00:33:11.324 --> 00:33:12.990
you can imagine how
you've been alerted.

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:14.980
You have all the history
of what's come before.

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:17.110
The user can read up,
understand the chat,

00:33:17.110 --> 00:33:19.730
and start getting involved in
the conversation very quickly.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:21.460
So that's obviously
quite an important,

00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:22.840
I would say,
prerequisite, as you

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:25.432
start getting into beyond
traditional just FAQs,

00:33:25.432 --> 00:33:27.640
and you start doing something
a little more involved.

00:33:27.640 --> 00:33:29.090
This would be something
that's quite nice to have,

00:33:29.090 --> 00:33:30.010
and now possible.

00:33:30.010 --> 00:33:30.770
REZA ROKNI: OK.

00:33:30.770 --> 00:33:31.360
Thank you.

00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:34.940
Can we go back to
the slides, please?

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:35.570
Fantastic.

00:33:40.523 --> 00:33:41.960
There we go.

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:44.929
So in terms of the architecture
patterns, there are two types.

00:33:44.929 --> 00:33:46.095
There's standard deployment.

00:33:46.095 --> 00:33:48.870
So there's a lot of things
that you can do just with

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:52.210
out-of-the-box integrations
with Dialogflow.

00:33:52.210 --> 00:33:54.550
That's both on the front
end and the back end.

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:57.260
We're using Firebase functions.

00:33:57.260 --> 00:34:00.494
In our example, we used this
very decoupled deployment

00:34:00.494 --> 00:34:01.910
because of the
complex environment

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:05.740
[INAUDIBLE] that we're in,
and some hybrid requirements.

00:34:05.740 --> 00:34:08.029
So just to cover the
standard architecture,

00:34:08.029 --> 00:34:10.920
the out-of-the-box integrations,
a couple of clicks and you get

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:11.940
things integrated.

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:14.010
It takes care of all
the things, like when

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:16.800
a user makes a call
to the front end,

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:19.830
the queries get sent
onto Dialogflow for you.

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:22.889
You have things like, you can
write simple Cloud Functions

00:34:22.889 --> 00:34:25.260
to deal with the
fulfillment side of things.

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:28.749
In terms of our architecture, if
we dive into what that actually

00:34:28.749 --> 00:34:31.040
means, that flow, let's start
picking this a little bit

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:31.710
apart.

00:34:31.710 --> 00:34:34.010
So first of all, the
fulfillment side of the house,

00:34:34.010 --> 00:34:35.460
this is a web call.

00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:37.770
So now, if you want
to decouple that,

00:34:37.770 --> 00:34:40.469
you can just stand up
a RESTful web service,

00:34:40.469 --> 00:34:42.780
and absorb these calls,
and give the response

00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:45.900
wherever you choose to
stand up your web service.

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:47.489
On the other side of the house--

00:34:47.489 --> 00:34:49.659
although we didn't
show it in the demo,

00:34:49.659 --> 00:34:51.540
we were using standard client--

00:34:51.540 --> 00:34:53.580
it would be important
for the client

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:57.180
to be outside of the
standard integrations.

00:34:57.180 --> 00:35:00.185
Because, for example, we
would want the authentication

00:35:00.185 --> 00:35:04.530
and authorization for the client
to be done within Citi using

00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:06.090
Citi's mechanisms.

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:09.450
And this means we end
up with a typology

00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:12.480
where we are going to have the
client be essentially something

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:16.380
that the user builds and
proxies to Dialogflow.

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:19.230
So this is going back to our
complex environment document.

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:21.850
So here, we have
that flow, right?

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:24.510
So we have a user that is--

00:35:24.510 --> 00:35:26.670
an application that
can use authentication

00:35:26.670 --> 00:35:30.480
and authorization from
within the environment that

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:31.860
exists within Citi.

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:35.340
It can then communicate,
through the hub service,

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:39.480
to all of these other systems.

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:42.480
This is the flowchart
of how this would work.

00:35:42.480 --> 00:35:45.750
If you imagine the client is
now a piece of code that's

00:35:45.750 --> 00:35:50.484
sitting in a container at
Citi, the user logs in,

00:35:50.484 --> 00:35:51.900
they're going to
do authentication

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:53.900
and authorization with the hub.

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:56.370
And the code in the hub,
we put into a container,

00:35:56.370 --> 00:35:58.350
which meant it was
easily portable

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:00.900
between our environments.

00:36:00.900 --> 00:36:03.930
The authorization, once
done, the hub-- the user

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:06.660
can then start
putting in requests.

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:08.540
That request is sent to the hub.

00:36:08.540 --> 00:36:12.150
The hub then takes that request,
piggybacks it onto another HTTP

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:14.430
call over to Dialogflow.

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:16.730
We get the response
from Dialogflow.

00:36:16.730 --> 00:36:18.450
The hub can respond
with, we're going

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:19.900
to be creating
the graph for you.

00:36:19.900 --> 00:36:21.900
If you imagine a trader
is typing on one screen,

00:36:21.900 --> 00:36:23.400
they've got-- and I've
been into those rooms.

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:24.566
They've got tons of screens.

00:36:24.566 --> 00:36:27.040
It's really cool.

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:29.850
Then, in the meantime, the
hub will also then do things

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:34.200
like make calls to BigQuery to
extract the tick data that was

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:37.380
available from Thomson Reuters.

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:39.510
Then, once it
receives that data,

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:42.150
we send that information
on to Firebase

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:44.445
to allow the clients
to be updated.

00:36:44.445 --> 00:36:46.951
So when I was calling
into Dialogflow,

00:36:46.951 --> 00:36:48.450
and you saw the
graph being updated,

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:52.180
that's what we were
using to achieve that.

00:36:52.180 --> 00:36:53.047
So, lessons learned.

00:36:53.047 --> 00:36:53.880
JAMES TROMANS: Yeah.

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:57.036
So I think, when you're
thinking about using something

00:36:57.036 --> 00:36:58.410
like Dialogflow
yourself, I mean,

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:00.076
the architecture that
we've come up with

00:37:00.076 --> 00:37:04.020
was something that I asked
Rez to help us with because we

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:07.110
have quite a lockdown network.

00:37:07.110 --> 00:37:09.930
We have a lot of existing
constraints and rules

00:37:09.930 --> 00:37:11.010
that we have to follow.

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:13.980
And so the hub architecture
really enabled us to work

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:17.549
within those requirements,
whereas out-of-the-box

00:37:17.549 --> 00:37:19.590
Dialogflow gives you a
lot of this functionality.

00:37:19.590 --> 00:37:21.460
If you're going to be
cloud native already

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:23.217
or it's a completely
greenfield project,

00:37:23.217 --> 00:37:24.800
there's a lot you
don't have to build.

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:26.970
So the hub gives
us that flexibility

00:37:26.970 --> 00:37:28.770
to take something
that we already have

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:29.820
and move towards
something that's

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:31.130
going to look like production.

00:37:31.130 --> 00:37:33.540
Whereas, if you were just
starting this from scratch,

00:37:33.540 --> 00:37:35.248
you would be able to
do this more simply.

00:37:35.248 --> 00:37:37.680
So you kind of have the
best of both worlds.

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:40.980
When we started
this, in my mind I

00:37:40.980 --> 00:37:42.810
had the idea of
what conversations

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:46.200
I wanted to encode into
the Dialogflow framework.

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:48.180
I had the lingo.

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:50.100
I knew the way this
was going to work.

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:51.480
So we kind of jumped in.

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:54.420
And there's me talking with Rez,
and we're going through this,

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:57.246
and we're getting excited with
how quickly and easily we're

00:37:57.246 --> 00:37:58.620
able to build some
of this stuff.

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:00.930
But of course, I'd
recommend, like with

00:38:00.930 --> 00:38:04.410
any architectural
design process, or even

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:07.450
your actual software
implementation process,

00:38:07.450 --> 00:38:09.360
you actually do sit down
with a pen and paper

00:38:09.360 --> 00:38:11.400
and work out what you want to
do first, rather than just jump

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:11.900
in.

00:38:11.900 --> 00:38:16.029
Because you need to make sure
that you make some assumptions

00:38:16.029 --> 00:38:17.820
that are the correct
ones at the beginning.

00:38:17.820 --> 00:38:19.530
Because otherwise, you're
going to end up unpicking some

00:38:19.530 --> 00:38:21.430
of the stuff you've done later.

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:23.509
So are your conversations
correct for Dialogflow

00:38:23.509 --> 00:38:24.300
in the first place?

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:24.730
OK.

00:38:24.730 --> 00:38:25.545
Let's say they are.

00:38:25.545 --> 00:38:27.920
You want to make sure you know
who the target audience is

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:30.990
for your chatbot,
like any product.

00:38:30.990 --> 00:38:33.590
And then, you want to work out,
for that target audience, what

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:35.310
are the number one things
that we're spending time

00:38:35.310 --> 00:38:37.010
doing now that we
don't want to do,

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:39.260
or that we can do a better
job of doing by automating?

00:38:39.260 --> 00:38:41.290
So what are your key use cases?

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:42.910
And then, you need
a persona, right?

00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:45.770
So in finance,
generally speaking,

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:48.041
people are doing a
lot of work every day.

00:38:48.041 --> 00:38:49.040
It's very transactional.

00:38:49.040 --> 00:38:50.260
It's quick, it's fast.

00:38:50.260 --> 00:38:52.710
There's not much
small talk, really.

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:55.000
And so you want this persona
to be quite efficient

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:57.250
so that you can move on and
do the next thing that you

00:38:57.250 --> 00:38:58.610
need to do.

00:38:58.610 --> 00:39:01.510
So then you want to sample--
you want to design things out,

00:39:01.510 --> 00:39:04.000
you want to test, and that's
the green section here.

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:06.190
So spending more time
doing that at the beginning

00:39:06.190 --> 00:39:09.925
is going to help you a lot
later on down the line.

00:39:09.925 --> 00:39:11.320
REZA ROKNI: OK.

00:39:11.320 --> 00:39:13.320
And actually, one thing
from that previous slide

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:14.861
that I found
particularly interesting

00:39:14.861 --> 00:39:17.910
is it's very easy, when
you're building out

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:19.860
the conversation
and the intents,

00:39:19.860 --> 00:39:24.900
to assume that other people will
ask it in the same kind of way.

00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:27.290
Being from London and
working with a lot of folks

00:39:27.290 --> 00:39:31.200
from the US, et cetera, I know
that to be very not true now.

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:35.280
And it's very useful to get
some intents up and running,

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:37.680
and then get colleagues-- and
I'd actually say colleagues

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:40.660
from different countries, if you
can-- to actually try it out.

00:39:40.660 --> 00:39:43.350
It's amazing the different kind
of variations that you get.

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:44.850
And one thing we
didn't show you was

00:39:44.850 --> 00:39:46.800
how easy it is to do
training with Dialogflow.

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:48.509
So you get one of
these sessions--

00:39:48.509 --> 00:39:49.800
it's almost like a jam session.

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:52.710
You sit down and everyone
tries to fool the system.

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:55.830
You find different ways of
actually asking the questions,

00:39:55.830 --> 00:39:57.240
and then use that to train it.

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:59.080
And when you're
doing the training,

00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:02.430
make sure that part
of the training

00:40:02.430 --> 00:40:05.580
hasn't exposed a different
branch of the conversation

00:40:05.580 --> 00:40:07.140
that you need to build in.

00:40:07.140 --> 00:40:09.300
So it's very easy
to start trying

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:11.890
to overload a single
branch of the conversation,

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:14.250
rather than saying, OK,
actually, the intent has now

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:16.260
changed, we should
create a separate intent

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.630
to cover that kind of flow.

00:40:18.630 --> 00:40:21.490
The other one from that first
one is the tail latency.

00:40:21.490 --> 00:40:24.090
So what you'll find is that
you'll very quickly get

00:40:24.090 --> 00:40:27.300
the chatbot up and running,
in terms of the standard flow

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:28.360
of the conversation.

00:40:28.360 --> 00:40:31.952
Spend just as much time on the
bits of the conversation that

00:40:31.952 --> 00:40:32.910
might be broken, right?

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:35.130
So if the user goes
down the wrong path,

00:40:35.130 --> 00:40:37.000
the context get
incorrectly set up.

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:38.250
Spend just as much time there.

00:40:38.250 --> 00:40:41.326
Because a couple of negative
experiences from a user,

00:40:41.326 --> 00:40:43.200
and then they're going
to be reluctant to use

00:40:43.200 --> 00:40:44.010
the chatbot again.

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:45.510
JAMES TROMANS: Just
on the training,

00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:49.230
I think one thing I should add
is when I started doing this,

00:40:49.230 --> 00:40:50.400
at first, you're like, OK.

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:52.792
Chart me euro dollar,
show me this, plot that.

00:40:52.792 --> 00:40:54.750
And you're kind of showing
a bunch of examples,

00:40:54.750 --> 00:40:56.460
and you're thinking, I
could just write this myself

00:40:56.460 --> 00:40:57.501
with regular expressions.

00:40:57.501 --> 00:40:58.620
What's the big deal?

00:40:58.620 --> 00:41:01.860
And that was how I came to
this, and I was like, mm.

00:41:01.860 --> 00:41:04.170
But what you quickly realize
is, through the workflow

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:07.159
that Dialogflow gives you, you
can quickly tag, like, hey,

00:41:07.159 --> 00:41:07.950
this is the intent.

00:41:07.950 --> 00:41:09.420
This is the entities.

00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:12.060
But you've given it maybe
half a dozen, or whatever--

00:41:12.060 --> 00:41:13.470
3, 4, 5.

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:16.499
And someone then uses
the tool in a way

00:41:16.499 --> 00:41:17.790
which you hadn't trained it on.

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:18.870
And that's when you
start to be like, ah,

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:20.400
that was my ah-ha
moment, when I was like,

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:22.110
oh, actually, it's going to
understand this thing that I

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:23.190
didn't train it on.

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:25.660
And subtly, and I think
quite importantly,

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:29.010
if you're into language,
understanding that something

00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:30.630
plot me-- whatever
that might be--

00:41:30.630 --> 00:41:33.200
and then the entities, you could
ask that in a different way.

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:34.700
You could ask that
like, eurodollar,

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:36.170
show me a chart of that.

00:41:36.170 --> 00:41:38.730
And Dialogflow gets robust
to these types of things,

00:41:38.730 --> 00:41:40.800
as long as you've
given a sensible amount

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:42.090
of seeded information.

00:41:42.090 --> 00:41:43.590
And that was one
of the key things

00:41:43.590 --> 00:41:45.214
that I really liked
about that the tool

00:41:45.214 --> 00:41:47.777
that, at first, I
didn't quite get.

00:41:47.777 --> 00:41:48.860
REZA ROKNI: Thanks, James.

00:41:48.860 --> 00:41:51.300
And the other piece,
when you're drawing out

00:41:51.300 --> 00:41:52.845
these conversations.

00:41:52.845 --> 00:41:56.310
Draw out some flowcharts using
the tools that Dialogflow has,

00:41:56.310 --> 00:41:58.290
in terms of like
slot filling, which

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.330
was where we asked
for currencies when

00:42:00.330 --> 00:42:01.420
one wasn't given.

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:03.060
Now, context, which
we didn't show you,

00:42:03.060 --> 00:42:05.520
allows you to store the
context of a conversation

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:06.707
as you branch.

00:42:06.707 --> 00:42:08.540
I think it was very,
very useful to actually

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:10.740
just pencil these
things out, and then

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:12.090
go and build the intents.

00:42:12.090 --> 00:42:13.770
What you'll probably find is,
as you're penciling these things

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:16.440
out, as well, you'll find some
branches that don't make sense,

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:17.580
and you'll fix them up.

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:20.430
So with that, some
final thoughts.

00:42:20.430 --> 00:42:21.160
James?

00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:21.352
JAMES TROMANS: Yeah.

00:42:21.352 --> 00:42:23.143
So hopefully, you guys
have got a good feel

00:42:23.143 --> 00:42:26.520
about how you can use Dialogflow
within existing applications,

00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:28.599
also within existing
infrastructure,

00:42:28.599 --> 00:42:30.640
depending on the company
that you're coming from.

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:33.270
And I think I'll loop this
back to the beginning, which

00:42:33.270 --> 00:42:36.090
is what seeded this initial
idea, which we actually

00:42:36.090 --> 00:42:37.800
haven't fully fleshed out yet.

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:41.040
But for us, because
we have so much data

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:42.840
and we are a large
bank, and we service

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:45.480
a lot of different
clients, we think

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:48.180
that one of our
unique benefits is

00:42:48.180 --> 00:42:50.580
that we can bring the
right type of data

00:42:50.580 --> 00:42:52.170
to the client when they need it.

00:42:52.170 --> 00:42:54.520
And so, for me, I was
thinking about, OK,

00:42:54.520 --> 00:42:56.940
what happens if both
an internal user comes

00:42:56.940 --> 00:42:59.160
to a trader who is
very busy, and they're

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:00.480
managing their portfolio.

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:02.740
And that user wants
to say, to the trader,

00:43:02.740 --> 00:43:04.670
why did the exchange
rate of this asset

00:43:04.670 --> 00:43:07.590
just spike over the
last 15 minutes?

00:43:07.590 --> 00:43:08.960
What caused that?

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:10.710
Now, we have all of
the trader information

00:43:10.710 --> 00:43:11.920
that happened up to that point.

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:14.200
We have all the economic
event data up to that point.

00:43:14.200 --> 00:43:16.074
We have all the market
data up to that point.

00:43:16.074 --> 00:43:18.085
It's not beyond the
realms of possibility,

00:43:18.085 --> 00:43:20.850
and we've been building this so
that you can programmatically

00:43:20.850 --> 00:43:23.970
produce a natural language
response to that query that

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:25.710
draws on all of this
data, and frames it

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:27.870
in such a way that
is human readable,

00:43:27.870 --> 00:43:29.100
and it's nice to read.

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:33.780
So we can say we're seeing a lot
of leveraged investors buying

00:43:33.780 --> 00:43:37.380
because the economic
release that just happened

00:43:37.380 --> 00:43:39.400
was weaker than we thought.

00:43:39.400 --> 00:43:41.580
And we're now at
the 94th percentile

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:43.420
rank for the month for
this exchange rate.

00:43:43.420 --> 00:43:46.264
And so we're seeing a lot of
momentum in that direction.

00:43:46.264 --> 00:43:48.180
You could produce that
report programmatically

00:43:48.180 --> 00:43:50.430
through the data that we have,
and you could imagine a user

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:52.425
asking for that, just
quickly on a chat, being

00:43:52.425 --> 00:43:53.508
like, why did this happen?

00:43:53.508 --> 00:43:55.199
You could respond
in chat in kind.

00:43:55.199 --> 00:43:57.240
And so for me, where I
want to take this project,

00:43:57.240 --> 00:43:58.710
is more down these lines.

00:43:58.710 --> 00:44:00.709
I think Dialogflow
is great for FAQs.

00:44:00.709 --> 00:44:02.250
It's great for just
the simple things

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:04.140
and, for sure, we'll be
looking at that, as well.

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:06.514
But I think there's a lot more
interesting, subtle things

00:44:06.514 --> 00:44:08.070
that you can do,
which create a sort

00:44:08.070 --> 00:44:10.729
of overall ecosystem of text.

00:44:10.729 --> 00:44:12.270
And of course, there
are some clients

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:14.380
who just want everything over
API, and that's fine, too.

00:44:14.380 --> 00:44:16.060
But for the people
that are in between,

00:44:16.060 --> 00:44:18.640
this is a great way to service
many, many more clients

00:44:18.640 --> 00:44:20.440
with high-quality content.

00:44:20.440 --> 00:44:22.277
So we've covered
a lot of ground.

00:44:22.277 --> 00:44:23.860
Hopefully, you got
a good feel for it.

00:44:23.860 --> 00:44:25.200
But that's it for me.

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:26.770
REZA ROKNI: And
just to say, there

00:44:26.770 --> 00:44:32.350
are some more sessions that are
available, running tomorrow.

00:44:32.350 --> 00:44:35.170
And again, thank you very much
for spending some time with us

00:44:35.170 --> 00:44:35.890
this morning.

00:44:35.890 --> 00:44:40.140
